WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.680
Quite a few times when
I've been standing at a bus stop

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:03.840
people have given me money,

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.720
mistakenly thinking
that I was begging

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.680
rather than just waiting on a bus.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:08.960
I was eight months pregnant,

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.400
I was in the street
and somebody stopped me

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:12.600
and pointed out I was pregnant.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.120
They then said to me,
"How did you get pregnant?"

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.360
So I did end up saying,

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.600
"I had sex with my husband,
how do you think I got pregnant?"

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.840
There are so many times when members
of the public come up to me and you,

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.480
don't they Ogg?
And you look at them don't you?

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:25.920
So I'm like what is she doing?
What is she looking at?

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.920
Is it something that I have
to be alert and aware of?

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.840
I feel so bad sometimes saying,

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.280
"Please don’t do that."
but it is really unhelpful.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.440
For many people disability can seem
to be a complex thing to talk about.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.560
Perhaps they fear saying the wrong
thing or seeming patronising.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.160
Two thirds of us are uncomfortable
when talking to disabled people

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.480
according to a survey done
by the disability charity Scope.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.520
A disability can be a broad range
of physical conditions,

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.200
chronic health problems
and mental health issues.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:01.200
Some disabilities are clearly visible
while others can't be seen.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.000
No!

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.640
I don't suffer from my disability,

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.480
rather I live with
or have cerebral palsy.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.400
Saying a disabled person
suffers from their disability

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.760
portrays quite a negative
image of a disabled person

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.720
rather than someone who just needs
adjustments or the right support

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.000
to be able to thrive.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.360
There's still a huge amount

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.400
of low-level discrimination
that disabled people face.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:28.960
So they think it's
amazing and wonderful

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.120
and aren't you incredible
because you're out and about,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.800
not something that just should
be an accepted part of society.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.240
And we're not all inspirational

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.240
just because we've got
out of bed in the morning.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.680
It is actually one of the
most patronising things

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.640
for a disabled person to experience

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:46.640
is for someone non-disabled to come
and say, "You're so inspirational."

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.680
Because if you say "why?"
they often don't have an answer.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.920
I'm a disabled person
not a person with a disability.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:58.560
Person with a disability is often
used by non-disabled people

00:01:58.640 --> 00:01:59.880
because they think it sounds nicer.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.080
But actually, for me, it's society
that makes my life different.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.680
It's steps, it's cobbles,
it's lifts that don't work.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.920
My disability is not
a separate part of me

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.680
that I can choose
to pick up or put down.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.920
I lost my sight six years ago.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.120
One time I was approaching
a kerb with Olga,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.920
she was on the pavement and I wasn't,

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.040
a gentleman came over
and gave her a stroke.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.280
It made her stop in her tracks a bit
and I was still in the road.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.120
Please don't distract her
because she's doing a job...

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.880
I'm not wheelchair-bound,
confined to or in a wheelchair,

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.720
rather, I just use my
wheelchair to get around.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:45.440
So I'm a wheelchair user
or someone using a wheelchair.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:51.160
Jokes about my speeding or whether
I've got a license are pretty weird

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.640
when you consider that my wheels
are essentially my legs.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.800
It's not about avoiding topics
or trying to say the right thing

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.640
rather just treating people as you
yourself would wish to be treated.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.480
Like many things in modern society

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.240
it's about asking
for a person's consent

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.200
and how they want things to go down

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.120
rather than just assuming.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:11.320
Stop and think how
you talk to anyone else

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.600
and use that when you
talk to a disabled person

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:14.800
just listen to what they say.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.560
What would be so great
is if people came up to me

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.760
and asked me if they
could stroke Olga.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:20.800
I think it's really important

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.000
to have that dialogue between me
and the person interacting with me,

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:30.520
because the audible world is
so important to a blind person.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.920
We don't need to be condescended
to or treated differently.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:37.840
We need to be respected
and supported.

